# Module-scoped exclusions: every pattern resolves relative to this directory, and
# none may use a parent traversal or an absolute path, so nothing here can reach the
# legacy estate that is the parity baseline.
#
# Four broad patterns are forbidden here because each would silently untrack a required
# deliverable: *.properties (the wrapper descriptor), application-*.yml (the committed
# Spring profiles), *.txt (the fixed-width golden fixtures and the banner) and *.json
# (the validation lookup tables and the metrics dashboard). Patterns are therefore
# written as narrowly as the artefact allows, anchored with a leading slash when the
# artefact only ever appears at the module root, and the highest-risk deliverables are
# re-included by name at the foot of the file. Git applies the last matching pattern, so
# those negations also act as a tripwire against a future broad addition.
#
# Extension globs are paired lowercase/UPPERCASE because the estate mixes extension
# casing; fixed names that a tool only ever emits in lowercase are matched as emitted.

# Build output. Anchored: Maven writes it at the module root only.
/target/

# Defensive only: Gradle is not this module's build tool and IDE compilation writes
# elsewhere, but none of these directories is ever a source.
/build/
/out/
/bin/

# The wrapper ships as scripts only, so no wrapper jar is committed. The wrapper
# DIRECTORY is deliberately not excluded: a negation cannot resurrect a file beneath an
# excluded directory, so excluding .mvn/ would make the descriptor negation below inert
# and break the hermetic build.
.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar
.mvn/.develocity/
.mvn/.gradle-enterprise/

# Residue of the release, versions, shade, flatten and buildnumber plugins. Each is a
# complete file name, so none can match the wrapper descriptor.
dependency-reduced-pom.xml
release.properties
pom.xml.releaseBackup
pom.xml.versionsBackup
pom.xml.next
pom.xml.tag
buildNumber.properties
.flattened-pom.xml

# Compiled classes, wherever a tool drops them.
*.class
*.CLASS

# Gate evidence is reproducible from a clean checkout plus one build, so no report is
# committed and no stale report can be mistaken for a current one. Coverage data and
# report trees already fall under /target/; they are named again because both locations
# are overridable from the command line and would then land outside it.
*.exec
*.EXEC
surefire-reports/
failsafe-reports/
/target/test-output/

# Vulnerability scan output: bound to verify, emits HTML, JSON and XML.
dependency-check-report.*

# Local vulnerability database. Regenerable, resolved beneath the user's home directory
# by default; these patterns cover a redirect into the module.
.dependency-check/
dependency-check-data/

# Local secrets. Every secret resolves from the environment with no fallback default, so
# a missing one fails startup; an ignore file cannot enforce that but is the last line of
# defence. Compose reads a .env file from this directory and interpolates the AWS keys and
# the database password from it, which makes a module-level .env the most likely place for
# a real credential to be committed by accident. Last matching pattern wins, so the
# template re-inclusion must follow the exclusions.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Keys, certificates and keystores. None is a source file in this module.
*.pem
*.PEM
*.key
*.KEY
*.p12
*.P12
*.pfx
*.PFX
*.jks
*.JKS
*.keystore
*.KEYSTORE
*.truststore
*.TRUSTSTORE
secrets/

# Developer overrides, which commonly carry real credentials. None of these can match a
# committed profile document: application-secret*.yml requires the literal segment
# "secret", and application-*.local.yml requires a ".local.yml" suffix that
# application-local.yml does not have.
application-local.properties
application-secret*.yml
application-secret*.yaml
application-*.local.yml
application-*.local.yaml

# Cloud credential files, anchored to the module root: an unanchored "credentials"
# pattern would also match a package directory of that name.
/credentials
/credentials.*
/aws.properties
.aws/

# IDE and editor state, so a pull request diff shows only the contributed change.
.idea/
*.iml
*.IML
*.ipr
*.IPR
*.iws
*.IWS

# Eclipse
.classpath
.project
.settings/
.factorypath
.apt_generated/

# Visual Studio Code
.vscode/

# NetBeans: private and generated subtrees only, since a shared nbproject would be
# a committed artefact.
nbproject/private/
nbbuild/
nbdist/
.nb-gradle/

# Editor scratch, patch and merge residue
*~
*.swp
*.SWP
*.swo
*.SWO
*.bak
*.BAK
*.orig
*.ORIG
*.rej
*.REJ

# Operating-system noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
desktop.ini

# Logging is structured JSON to the console only, so these are defensive: they cover a
# developer who enables a file destination locally.
*.log
*.LOG
*.log.*
/logs/

# JVM fatal-error, compiler-replay and heap-dump output.
hs_err_pid*
replay_pid*
*.hprof
*.HPROF
heapdump*

# Core dumps, narrowed on purpose: a bare core.* would wrongly exclude core.json or
# core.yml, whereas a Linux core dump is named core or core followed by the process id.
/core
core.[0-9]*

# Local stack state. It lives in named Docker volumes today, so nothing appears in the
# working tree; these patterns cover switching a named volume to a bind mount. The
# read-only assets the stack mounts - config/ and localstack/ - are never excluded.
postgres-data/
localstack-data/
prometheus-data/
grafana-data/
volume/
volumes/
.localstack/

# Required committed artefacts, re-included by name. Last matching pattern wins, so these
# also neutralise a future broad pattern added above them. They are effective because no
# directory pattern here excludes any of their parents.

# The wrapper descriptor. Losing it breaks the hermetic build.
!.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties

# The four Spring profile documents. Three are application resources; the
# test profile is a TEST resource, because it binds to endpoints
# Testcontainers only publishes while a test JVM is running and must never
# ship inside the deployable artifact. Its negation therefore names
# src/test/resources - naming src/main/resources would protect a path that
# does not exist and leave the real file unprotected. The production profile
# is safe to commit precisely because it holds no secret value, only
# unresolved environment references with no fallback default.
!src/main/resources/application.yml
!src/main/resources/application-local.yml
!src/main/resources/application-prod.yml
!src/test/resources/application-test.yml

# Startup banner and logging configuration.
!src/main/resources/banner.txt
!src/main/resources/logback-spring.xml

# Byte-parity fixtures: fixed-width golden output at the legacy record widths, so they are
# sources rather than generated artefacts.
!src/test/resources/fixtures/input/**
!src/test/resources/fixtures/expected/**
